[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 746,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 576,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114250,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 124,
        "headingId": 576,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Inflammatory collateral cysts (ICCs) are inflammatory cysts on the buccal or distobuccal aspect of the roots of partially or recently erupted teeth.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 747,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 577,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114251,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 124,
        "headingId": 577,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 748,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 578,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114252,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 124,
        "headingId": 578,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>DA05.Y Other specified cysts of oral or facial-neck region</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 749,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 579,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114253,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 124,
        "headingId": 579,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> mandibular infected buccal cyst; inflammatory paradental cyst.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 750,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 580,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114254,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 124,
        "headingId": 580,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Paradental cyst (PC); mandibular buccal bifurcation cyst (MBBC)</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 751,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 581,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114255,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 124,
        "headingId": 581,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>PCs account for 60% of all ICCs and are found on the distobuccal aspect of mandibular third molars. MBBCs (35%) are found on the buccal aspect of mandibular first or second molars and are often bilateral (in as many as 25% of cases). Rare cases of ICC (4%) arise at other sites {15128056; 2760852}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 752,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 582,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114256,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 124,
        "headingId": 582,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>PCs are associated with chronic pericoronitis but are often symptomless at presentation. MBBCs may be infected with pain, tenderness, and suppuration. The tooth is vital and often tilted buccally with deep periodontal pockets.</p>\r\n<h5>Imaging</h5>\r\n<p>ICCs appear as corticated radiolucencies overlying the buccal or distobuccal aspect of the tooth. The lamina dura is intact, and the follicular space surrounding a partially erupted tooth is normal {12180213}.</p>\r\n<p>MBBCs may extend to the lower border of the mandible {15128056}, and they often show a periosteal reaction, with laminated new bone formation.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 753,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 583,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114257,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 124,
        "headingId": 583,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>ICCs account for 5% of odontogenic cysts and have a male predilection (70%). The average age is 30&nbsp;years for PC and 9&nbsp;years (first molar) or 17&nbsp;years (second molar) for MBBC {1065342; 15128056}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 754,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 584,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114258,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 124,
        "headingId": 584,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>ICCs are caused by inflammation in the pericoronal tissues. Cyst formation may be exacerbated by food impaction or by an enamel projection on the buccal aspect of the tooth {1065342; 12180213}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 755,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 585,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114259,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 124,
        "headingId": 585,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>ICCs are pocket cysts caused by dilation of the pericoronal tissues and lined by sulcular or junctional epithelium derived from the reduced enamel epithelium {1065342; 26116019}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 762,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 592,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114260,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 124,
        "headingId": 592,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>If removed intact, the cyst appears as a sac-like mass attached to the buccal or distobuccal aspect of the coronal third of the tooth root.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 756,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 586,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114261,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 124,
        "headingId": 586,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>ICC is indistinguishable from radicular cyst. It has an inflamed wall lined by hyperplastic non-keratinized epithelium. Cholesterol deposition and foamy histiocytes are common. The cyst may be open and continuous with the pericoronal or gingival tissues.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 757,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 587,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114262,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 124,
        "headingId": 587,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 758,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 588,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114263,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 124,
        "headingId": 588,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 761,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 591,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114264,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 124,
        "headingId": 591,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> associated with partially or recently erupted vital tooth; radiolucency distinct from dental follicle; intact lamina dura; non-keratinized epithelium.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 759,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 589,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114265,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 124,
        "headingId": 589,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 760,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 590,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 114266,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 124,
        "headingId": 590,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Cysts are enucleated. Third molars associated with PC are removed. Molars associated with MBBC can be conserved and usually erupt normally.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T10:14:56"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]